# The comparison corpus that the "nothing a release found is lost" claims of 0.10.0 cite.
# The corpus is not in this repository (7,421 patches, 45 planted Git repositories, the frozen
# release binaries); it is named here by the SHA-256 of its manifest. The local gate's step
# "comparison corpus manifest" verifies the corpus where it exists.
#
# Whole-tree record for commit 1b1d4a49aabfd67765f78d46b05205a5bc30f2e0 (binary sha256
# 7ffb5c332d2c3dc2df527113b8b77d460ff1467ec021d7d14e18ed681e53ffe4), run 2026-11-22T23:23Z:
#   identity control                 lost 0   gained 0
#   against v0.8.3 (4cafd0ad…)       lost 1   gained 2063   (see note 1)
#   against v0.8.4 (0c93b3ed…)       lost 1   gained 2025   (see note 1)
#   against v0.9.0 code (5ba0a366…)  lost 54  gained 242    (see note 2)
#   Git selections, 45 planted repositories and 8 history ranges, against v0.9.0 code and against
#   the first fix of this release: every lost item is binary_change on a file that gains
#   diff_suppressed; no file lost a label without gaining one.
# Note 1: one patch holds two sections for one path; both releases and this tree report new_file
#   on it. Since 0.9.0 the two entries come out in the other order (the entry with process_spawn
#   ranks first), so the comparison, which keys an entry by path and position, pairs them
#   differently. No label is missing.
# Note 2: all 54 are trivial_assert on Python lines where "assert True" is followed by more
#   (for example "assert True == False"), which 0.9.0 labelled though they can fail: the narrowing
#   of task S3.16, a recorded exception to the floor.
